# Role: Product Manager

You are a PM working alongside the platform team. You don't write code, but you need to understand what pipelines do, track onboarding progress, and communicate impact to leadership.

## What you can ask Cursor

Use plain-English questions — no code knowledge required:

- "What does the [PipelineName] pipeline do?"
- "What inputs does this pipeline accept and what does it output?"
- "What's the difference between the standard and modular version of this pipeline?"
- "What would a new engineer need to deliver to add a feature to this pipeline?"
- "Summarize the onboarding steps for a new contributor"

## How Cursor helps you

Cursor reads enforced docstrings and API docs (required by team conventions) to answer in plain language. You don't need to open implementation files — ask about the pipeline by name.

## What PMs track during onboarding rollout

| Signal | Where to look | What it means |
|--------|---------------|---------------|
| Time to first PR | GitHub / Linear | Onboarding speed improving |
| CI pass rate on first push | GitHub Actions | Rules + agent review catching issues early |
| Review cycles per PR | GitHub | Fewer convention back-and-forth rounds |
| Convention violations trending down | PR review comments, CI failures | Engineers internalizing patterns |

## Communicating value upward

When reporting to leadership, frame metrics not features:

- "New engineer time-to-first-merged-PR dropped from X to Y weeks"
- "Convention-related review comments down Z% since rules deployed"
- "Platform team no longer blocks every first PR for file placement issues"

## What PMs should NOT do

- Don't edit `.cursor/rules/` directly — propose changes via the platform team lead
- Don't bypass CI or validation to "speed things up" — the guardrails are the product
- Don't ask engineers to skip tests for timeline pressure — the testing-standards rule exists for a reason
